34,2,,status,Misaki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,Midori,,"Thank you for the information. I'm quite concerned to see that agency costs are rising while vacancies persist. Also, the failure of the joint clinic suggests we must be very careful about who owns the long-term responsibility.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"It is interesting to note that the call-back trial actually reduced readmissions for elderly patients, yet it was discontinued. It seems we often abandon programs that show genuine promise before they are fully integrated.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"The staffing data is worrying. We have significant vacancies in ward nursing and a loss of physiotherapists, though midwifery has some slack. We must ensure any new program doesn't further burden the already strained teams.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shion,,"The financial picture is bleaker than I expected. Our development budget has shrunk by a quarter, and we're now paying for floor space from it. Furthermore, off-site contacts earn us nothing. We cannot afford a program that doesn't generate revenue.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It seems we all have things to worry about. I've found that our rooms and parking are already quite full, and our booking system misses so much of the hard work being done. We must be careful.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shion, I understand the need for revenue, but if we choose a program that fails due to poor ownership—like that joint clinic—we waste the little budget we have left. We need a clear, sustainable structure.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, I agree. The call-back trial proved that simple interventions can save patients from returning to the hospital, yet it vanished. We should avoid creating another ""ghost"" program that disappears without a trace.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Jonathan, I agree. If we implement a program without considering who will actually manage the workload, it will either fail or burn out the staff. We cannot ignore the current vacancies in nursing and therapy.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Midori, ownership is a management issue, but insolvency is a structural one. If we ignore the fact that off-site work earns zero tariff, we aren't being sustainable; we're being delusional. We need a program that stays on-site.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, I understand your concern for the budget, but if we bring more people on-site when the parking is already full by eleven, I fear we may simply seize up. We must be very gentle with our space.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Shizue, that's a very fair point. If we can't physically accommodate the program, it won't matter how well-funded it is. We need a solution that balances revenue, staffing, and our actual physical capacity.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shion, if we prioritize on-site revenue but ignore the lessons of the call-back trial, we risk investing in something that looks good on a ledger but fails the patient. We must ensure this program is durable.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Shion, while on-site revenue is important, we cannot ignore that some teams are at a breaking point. If we push a program onto a department already struggling with vacancies, the cost of burnout will outweigh any financial gain.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, burnout is a variable, but a bankrupt budget is a constant. If we choose a program that drains our shrinking funds without a return, we'll have no money left to hire the staff you're so worried about.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, you speak with such passion for the numbers, and I understand. But if we ignore the invisible work that the booking system misses, we might accidentally break the very things that are currently keeping us afloat.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Midori,,"It seems we are all highlighting different but equally critical risks. To move forward, we must find a program that is financially viable, physically possible, and has a clear, committed owner to prevent it from collapsing.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. Let us look for a candidate that respects our staff's limits and our physical space, while ensuring the benefits to the patients are permanent rather than temporary.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Misaki,,"I agree. We need a program that doesn't just look good on paper, but is actually feasible given our current staffing gaps. Let's see which candidates align with these constraints.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shion,,Let's stop the vague agreements. We have a shrinking budget and a revenue gap for off-site work. I will vote for the candidate that maximizes on-site billing and minimizes capital expenditure. Logic must prevail over sentiment.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"Let us all take a deep breath. We each hold a piece of the puzzle, and only by fitting them together can we find a path that is kind to our staff and our patients.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
