60,3,1,talk,Midori,,"Looking at the options, I'm very concerned about Option D. A joint program between midwifery and pediatrics risks the same ownership collapse we saw with the joint clinic. We need a program with a single, clear point of responsibility.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, I agree. Furthermore, Option A reminds me of the call-back trial and the shared nursing round. While the intent is noble, the high failure rate of home visits due to access issues makes it a risky investment.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"@Jonathan, I agree. Option A would put more pressure on the ward nursing staff who are already struggling with vacancies. Option B is also problematic, as we are losing physiotherapists to city posts.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, if Option B is a staffing risk, then Option A is a financial disaster. Home visits earn zero tariff. We cannot waste a shrinking budget on a program that generates no revenue and costs us in travel.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@Shion, you are quite right about the costs. But I also worry that Option B would bring too many people to our already crowded parking lot. Perhaps Option C is a gentler way to help our patients?","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Midori,,"@Shizue, I think Option C is a very strong candidate. It focuses on coordination rather than adding more physical traffic or relying on risky joint-department ownership, which should make it more sustainable in the long run.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Shizue, I agree. Option C avoids the pitfalls of the failed home-visit initiatives and doesn't seem to demand the same physical space as Option B. It offers a way to provide lasting stability for the patients.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Midori, @Jonathan, I agree. Option C is the most feasible. It doesn't rely on the strained nursing or therapy teams, and it avoids the staffing gaps that would make Options A and B fail.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Misaki, you're all agreeing too quickly. Option C is coordination, which often means more off-site communication and zero tariff. However, compared to the blatant waste of Option A, it is the only logical choice left.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shizue,,It is heartening to see us finding common ground. Option C seems to be a path that respects our limits while still caring for those in need. It feels like a very peaceful choice for everyone.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Midori,,"I'm glad we've reached a consensus. Option C avoids the structural risks of joint ownership and the physical constraints of our site, making it the most reliable choice for our patients and staff.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"It is a relief to find a solution that avoids the cycle of temporary trials. By focusing on coordination, we can create a durable system that truly supports the patient without overextending our fragile resources.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Misaki,,I agree. Option C is the only one that doesn't risk collapsing due to our current staffing shortages. It is the most responsible choice for the wellbeing of our remaining clinical teams.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Midori, @Jonathan, @Misaki, your desperation for a ""safe"" choice is showing. While Option C isn't a revenue engine, it's the only one that doesn't actively bleed money or space. I'll concede to the logic of elimination.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Jonathan"",""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"It is a wonderful feeling when we all come together like this. Let us move forward with Option C, knowing we have looked after both our patients and the people who care for them.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Midori,,"I believe we have made a thoughtful and balanced decision. Option C protects our staff, respects our physical limits, and ensures a sustainable structure. I am happy to cast my vote for it.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I agree. Option C provides a stable framework that avoids the pitfalls of our past failures. It is a sustainable choice that honors both our resources and our patients. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Misaki,,I agree. Option C is the only viable path that doesn't place an impossible burden on our already exhausted staff. It is the most realistic and sustainable choice. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shion,,"Since you've all decided to prioritize safety over profit, I'll follow the path of least resistance. Option C is the only one that isn't a financial or spatial catastrophe. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shizue,,I am so glad we could find a way to be kind to one another and our hospital. Option C is a gentle choice that fits our home. I also vote for Option C.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
